Date: Tue, 26 Oct 93 22:01:41 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com (Hal Finney)
Subject: Bet 5000

Congratulations to James Donald for coming up with a concrete example
of a calculation to test his pending bet with Mike Price.  I don't know
enough physics to judge his calculations, but on the surface they look
reasonable, and it will be interesting to see what Mike says.  It would
certainly be exciting to see such a large bet put to the test.

The little bit of understanding I have of James' program does make
me somewhat skeptical that it will work.  The impression I have is that
he starts his system off in a superposition of all possible bit patterns.
Then as time goes forward he increases the energy in those patterns with
small deviations from the constraints while decreasing those with large
numbers of deviations.  In the end, only the solution with no deviations
is left.

As I said, my understanding is pretty shaky, but if this is what is happening
I'd be concerned by the large number of states.  With 80 bits we'd have
2^80 states, and the amount of energy in any one state is so tiny that I
would think that noise would be an overwhelming problem.  This is just
an intuition, though, and I will be very interested to see how it all
comes out.

In any case, James is to be congratulated for the obvious expenditure of
his own time and energy in coming up with a concrete problem, one which
is interesting in its own right.

Hal Finney
