27 October 1993
Evening Standard (London)
p 5


How computers will be smarter than humans
by TONY MAGUIRE
Industrial Editor


THE man known to a generation of computer scientists
as the mastermind of artificial intelligence today
predicted computers will evolve beyond humans.

Marvin Minsky, co-founder of the Massachusetts Artificial
Intelligence Laboratory, said humans may eventually be able
to "download" their personalities into computers, thereby
becoming smarter and more reliable.

The veteran scientist pours scorn on critics who say computers
can never become truly conscious, in an interview in the
November edition of Scientific American published today.

The mystery of consciousness is "trivial" says Mr Minsky.
"I've solved it, and I don't understand why people don't
listen."

According to Mr Minsky, consciousness involves one part
of the mind monitoring the behaviour of other parts.  This
function requires little more than short-term memory, or a
"low grade system for keeping records".

Some computer programmes already allow their processing
steps to be retraced and in this sense are "extremely
conscious".

The mind is a "tractor trailer" rolling on many wheels
but its functions are capable of being copied and overtaken
by computers.

His work centres on the belief that "you can build a
mind from many parts, each mindless by itself."

Mr Minsky's most bizarre claim is that the ability to
download personalities could extend life.  He envisages
making copies of himself that could undergo experiences
he would otherwise shun.  "I regard religious experience
as very risky, because it can destroy your brain.  But if
I had a backup copy..."

Mr Minsky split with the other co-founder of the Massachusetts
laboratory John McCarthy when they argued over future
definitions of artificial intelligence.

Mr McCarthy, who continued his research at his own
laboratory at Stanford University, favours the theory
of artificial intelligence based on logic.

Mr Minsky favours another view of a human as The Emotion
Machine, the title of his next book.  Although he is
confident computers can take intelligence beyond the
limits of the brain he admits some aspects have proved
harder to reproduce than expected.

